  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nA trough digging into the front range of the Rockies today will\nhelp two shortwaves phase. A strong early season low pressure\nsystem forms and moves into the Great Lakes region Thursday\nnight into Friday. A warm front lifts into the northeast\nThursday night with rain overspreading the area. There is still\nsome uncertainty if the warm front can lift all the way through\nour region into northern NY putting us completely in the warm\nsector or if the front may stall near the I-90 corridor. If we\nare in the warm sector, strong warm air advection drives 850 mb\ntemperatures to around 15C so areas that see breaks of sun may\nwarm into the mid 70s. Full sun may drive temperatures into the\nupper 70s but given the moisture in place in forecast soundings,\nodds are there will be plenty of clouds. Showers and drizzle may\nbe persistent as well in the warm sector. \n\nGiven the cloud cover and fairly stable warm sector, severe\nthunderstorms are not anticipated as the cap will be tough to\nbreak. Shear is in place, with 0-6 km shear around 50 knots. The\nmain trigger for any storms will be the frontal passage in the\nafternoon so there is a chance that a few thunderstorms along\nthe cold front could become severe with strong wind gusts being\nthe main concern. NEPA would have the best chance at getting\nbreaks of sun and some better instability. SPC has a marginal\nrisk just to the south and it could be expanded northward though\ntodays model trends have been to a slightly weaker low.\n\nPost frontal, the 850 mb temperatures drop nearly 25C from\naround 15C above to -7C to -9C. Lake effect rain showers quickly\ndevelop for much of the Southern Tier and Northward. It does\nlook like towards Sunrise Saturday, as the 850 mb temperatures\nnear their lowest readings, that some wet snow will mix in with\nthe lake effect rain at elevations above 800 feet or so. With\nthe lake effect winding down by then as subsidence takes over\nwith high pressure building in, no accumulation is expected.\nSaturday will be windy under the strong cold air advection, with\nwinds bumped up over the NBM. BUFKIT soundings hint that gusts\ncould approach 40 mph though that will likely be in the higher\nterrain though gusts over 30 are likely region wide. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nSaturday night into Sunday is looking cold as surface high\npressure builds in. Depending if we can completely clear from\nsome lake effect clouds, we could radiate out well into the 20s\nfor much of the area. Even so, it is likely we see the first\nwidespread killing freeze. Sunday and beyond, upper level\nridging builds back in and 500 mb heights rise back above 580\ndm. offshore flow and slow moisture return will also keep us dry\nthrough most of next week.",
